## Summary
The opposing actions of polycomb (PcG) and trithorax group (trxG) gene products maintain essential gene expression patterns during Drosophila development. PcG proteins are thought to establish repressive chromatin structures, but the mechanisms by which this occurs are not known. Polycomb repressive complex 1 (PRC1) contains several PcG proteins and inhibits chromatin remodeling by trxG-related SWI/SNF complexes. We have defined a functional core of PRC1 by reconstituting a stable complex using four recombinant PcG proteins. One subunit, PSC, can also inhibit chromatin remodeling on its own. These PcG proteins create a chromatin structure that has normal nucleosome organization and is accessible to nucleases but excludes hSWI/SNF.
## Introduction
The products of the PcG and trxG genes of Drosophila melanogaster comprise a memory system that maintains gene expression patterns across cell division and is essential for normal development (Kennison, 1995; Pirrotta, 1998; Simon, 1995). The PcG/trxG system is conserved in mammals where these proteins have also been implicated in control of cell proliferation and oncogenesis (Caldas and Aparicio, 1999). PcG proteins maintain silencing of repressed genes, while trxG proteins maintain expression of activated genes. These proteins are thought to function in complexes that exert their opposing effects on gene expression through chromatin structure. Consistent with this idea, several trxG proteins are found in the Drosophila homolog of the SWI/ SNF ATP-dependent chromatin remodeling complex (BRM complex) (Dingwall et al., 1995; Kal et al., 2000; Papoulas et al., 1998). In contrast, the molecular mechanisms by which PcG complexes influence chromatin structure have remained elusive, in part because of a lack of in vitro systems for studying them.
A common theme with regulatory complexes is that they contain a small number of “core” subunits whose activity is targeted and regulated via additional subunits. The targeting and regulatory activities can often be separated into mechanistically distinct steps. Identification of the core subunits is essential to understanding the basic molecular mechanisms by which these complexes function, and, subsequently, how each subunit contributes to regulatory events. For example, the human homologs of the trxG proteins BRM and MOR can reconstitute the core activity of hSWI/SNF (Phelan et al., 1999), and considerable mechanistic insight into chromatin remodeling has been gained by studying the effects of these isolated remodeling factors on chromatin templates (Kadam et al., 2000; Phelan et al., 2000).
In vivo, the PcG and trxG are targeted to appropriate genes by complex regulatory elements termed trithorax or polycomb response elements (TRE/PREs). Because most PcG and trxG proteins do not seem to have sequence-specific DNA binding activities, it is unclear how they are targeted to these elements. One hypothesis is that DNA binding factors interact with trxG and/or PcG complexes to recruit them to target genes. Indeed, the SWI/SNF complex is targeted by multiple transcription factors that are not stoichiometric members of the complex and are not required for the remodeling activity of the complex (i.e., Kadam et al., 2000). The finding that artificial recruitment of PcG proteins is sufficient to establish PcG repression in vivo (Bunker and Kingston, 1994; Muller, 1995; Poux et al., 2001; van der Vlag et al., 2000) suggests that repression and targeting mechanisms can be separated.
PRC1, a PcG protein containing complex purified from Drosophila embryos, can block chromatin remodeling by hSWI/SNF (Shao et al., 1999), which suggests that PcG complexes might directly antagonize the action of trxG complexes. How individual PcG proteins contribute to the inhibition of remodeling, however, is not known. To establish a defined in vitro system for studying how PcG proteins interact with the basic components of chromatin and how repression can subsequently be targeted to specific genes, we sought to identify the functional core of PRC1.
Extensive evidence suggests that the PcG proteins polyhomeotic (PH), posterior sex combs (PSC), and polycomb (Pc) function together in a complex. These three proteins colocalize at many sites on polytene chromosomes (DeCamillis et al., 1992; Martin and Adler, 1993; Rastelli et al., 1993) and show strong genetic (Campbell et al., 1995; Cheng et al., 1994) and biochemical (Franke et al., 1992; Kyba and Brock, 1999; Shao et al., 1999) interactions that are also conserved in mammals (Alkema et al., 1997; Gunster et al., 1997). Studies of mammalian PcG proteins indicate that a fourth protein, Ring1, interacts with homologs of Pc, PH, and PSC (Satijn et al., 1997; Schoorlemmer et al., 1997). Furthermore, Drosophila PRC1 contains apparently stoichiometric amounts of Pc, PH, PSC (Shao et al., 1999), and dRING1, and does not contain large amounts of any other PcG proteins (Saurin et al., 2001). These studies raised the possibility that these four proteins form the core of PRC1.
