## Results
## PH, PSC, Pc, and dRING1 Copurify in an Active PRC1 Core Complex
To assemble an active recombinant complex of PcG proteins that could be purified in large amounts and used for detailed functional analyses, Sf9 cells were coinfected with Flag-PH, Pc, PSC, and dRING1. FlagPH and associated proteins were purified by affinity chromatography. Pc, PSC, and dRING1 were purified quantitatively with Flag-PH, as shown in Figures 1A and 1B. If Sf9 cells were coinfected with PSC, PH, and Pc with either PSC or PH carrying the Flag epitope, the three proteins copurified but the eluted fractions contained significant excess of the epitope-tagged subunit, suggesting dRING1 is important for stable complex formation. Purification of Flag-PH from cells expressing FlagPH, dRING1, and Pc did not result in purification of a complex, but mainly of Flag-PH (data not shown). Thus, Pc, PH, PSC, and dRING1 formed the most stable complex, which we will refer to as PCC (PRC1 core complex) for simplicity. PCC fractions invariably contain a fifth, 70 kDa protein (asterisk in Figure 1A); this protein may be an HSC70 homolog from Sf9 cells since it is immunoreactive with antibodies to HSP70 (mAb MA3-006 from Affinity Bioreagents Inc.; data not shown). HSC70 may interact specifically with PcG proteins since HSC proteins copurify with PcG proteins in PRC1 (Saurin et al., 2001) and mutations in HSC4 enhance Pc phenotypes (Mollaaghababa et al., 2001); alternatively, it may associate with the complex simply because the PcG proteins are overexpressed.
To determine whether PCC shares the ability of PRC1 to block chromatin remodeling by hSWI/SNF (Shao et al., 1999), we tested PCC in the plasmid supercoiling assay originally used to define PRC1 activity. When incubated with a nucleosomal plasmid array and topoisomerase I, hSWI/SNF induces an ATP-dependent decrease in negative supercoiling, which can be identified on agarose gels as a change in the distribution of plasmid topoisomers (Kwon et al., 1994) (Figure 1C, lanes 1 and 2). PRC1 blocks the ability of hSWI/SNF to alter plasmid topology when preincubated with the template, but not when added simultaneously with hSWI/SNF (Shao et al., 1999). We found that preparations of PCC inhibit remodeling in this assay in a preincubation-dependent manner that mimics the activity of PRC1 (Figure 1C, lanes 3 and 4).
We purified each component of PCC to determine whether any individual protein shared functional characteristics with PCC or PRC1 (Figure 1D). One subunit, PSC, also inhibited chromatin remodeling in the plasmid supercoiling assay in a manner that was strictly dependent on preincubation (Figure 1C, lanes 5 and 6). Preparations of Pc, PH, and dRING1 were less active than
PSC in this assay (data not shown; note that we cannot conclude that these other isolated subunits are inactive, see below). We conclude that PCC and at least one of its subunits share the ability of PRC1 to inhibit chromatin remodeling.
PCC and PSC Efficiently Inhibit Chromatin Remodeling of Nucleosomal Arrays but Not of Mononucleosomes
To compare the relative efficiency of PRC1, PCC, and PSC, we developed a quantifiable assay for inhibition based on restriction enzyme accessibility. Assembly of DNA into chromatin blocks the ability of restriction enzymes to digest DNA at nucleosomal sites (Polach and Widom, 1995), but this decrease in accessibility can be counteracted by ATP-dependent chromatin remodeling factors such as hSWI/SNF (Logie and Peterson, 1997) (e.g., first two lanes of Figure 2B). Nucleosomal arrays were assembled by the use of templates consisting of two sets of five 5S nucleosome positioning sequences that flank DNA sufficient to assemble two nucleosomes, one of which is predicted to overlap a unique HhaI site (Figure 2A). The extent of digestion of the HhaI site in a chromatin template exposed to hSWI/SNF was used to quantify inhibition of chromatin remodeling by PcG proteins.
PRC1 blocks the ATP-dependent stimulation of restriction enzyme digestion by hSWI/SNF when preincubated with the template (8 nM nucleosomes) at a concentration of approximately 1 nM (Figures 2B and 2C). Under the same conditions, approximately 2–4 nM PCC also inhibits chromatin remodeling, suggesting the core complex is up to $50\%$ as efficient as PRC1 (Figures 2B and 2C). Note that we are unable to quantify the active concentration of PRC1, and therefore the concentration of PRC1 required for inhibition should be regarded as an upper limit (see legend to Figure 2). PSC inhibits chromatin remodeling at higher concentrations than PCC (Figures 2B and 2C). The finding that HhaI digestion is not completely blocked and the requirement for preincubation in order to observe full inhibition (data not shown) indicate that PcG proteins are not inhibiting the restriction enzyme; this was further addressed by examining the effect of PcG proteins on digestion by several different restriction enzymes (see below). Preparations of dRING1 did not inhibit remodeling in this protocol, while Pc and PH showed variable activity (data not shown). Thus, both PSC and PCC inhibit chromatin remodeling and are nearly as efficient as PRC1.
PcG preparations could inhibit remodeling through interactions with the nucleosomal array, by directly inhibiting hSWI/SNF function, or by a combination of these mechanisms. We previously found that PRC1 did not efficiently inhibit remodeling of mononucleosomes (Shao et al., 1999). This suggested that the complex might require a nucleosomal array substrate, but we were unable to obtain the high concentrations of PRC1 required to rigorously assess this finding. Because we can purify large amounts of concentrated PCC and PSC, we were able to test whether these proteins can inhibit remodeling of mononucleosomes over a wide range of protein concentrations in excess of nucleosomes. We titrated PCC and PSC into a restriction enzyme accessibility assay for hSWI/SNF remodeling of mononucleosomes assembled on a 155 bp DNA fragment. PCC and PSC had no effect on remodeling of mononucleosomes at concentrations greater than those required to completely inhibit remodeling of arrays, and PSC inhibited remodeling only when present at greater than 20-fold excess over template (Figure 2D). These results suggest that PCC or PSC inhibit chromatin remodeling by interacting with the substrate and that these proteins have specific substrate requirements not present in mononucleosomes, such as linker DNA or multiple contiguous nucleosomes. These experiments also confirm that concentrations of PCC and PSC that inhibit remodeling of nucleosomal arrays do not directly inhibit hSWI/SNF activity.
## Inhibition by Isolated PSC
Purified PSC preparations inhibit remodeling; it was possible, however, that low levels of Sf9 PRC1 subunits that copurified with PSC contributed to inhibition. By varying nucleosome concentration, we found that 8 nM PSC is able to inhibit remodeling of at least 32 nM nucleosomes, which was the highest concentration that could be attained in our reactions (Figure 3A). The low molar ratios of PSC to nucleosomes in these experiments argue against a role for small amounts of contaminating proteins in inhibition of remodeling. To further examine this issue, immunoaffinity isolated PSC fractions were further purified by size fractionation. In preliminary glycerol gradient and gel filtration experiments, PSC behaved as a large entity, likely due to aggregation or association with other proteins. To disaggregate PSC and separate contaminating proteins, PSC was dialyzed into 2 M urea, $300~\mathsf{m M}$ KCl, and fractionated on a glycerol gradient under these conditions (Figure 3B). PSC fractionated as an approximately 200 kDa protein, away from the 70 kDa contaminant (asterisk in Figure 3B) which may correspond to HSC70, as observed in PCC. Following dialysis to remove urea, fractions were tested for antiremodeling activity (Figure 3C). The extent of inhibition of remodeling correlated closely with the amount of PSC in the fractions (Figure 3D) as determined by titrations on silver-stained protein gels and Western blots (data not shown). The specific activity of the gradient fractions was similar to that of the input immunoaffinity fraction. Gradient-isolated PSC also inhibited chromatin remodeling in the plasmid supercoiling assay (data not shown). Thus, PSC does not require other proteins to inhibit chromatin remodeling.
## Functional Characterization of PCC and PSC
The experiments reported above demonstrate that the stable PcG complex PCC and the single subunit PSC share the ability of PRC1 to inhibit remodeling. The definition of this minimal system and the ability to obtain large quantities of highly purified PCC and PSC allows a more detailed characterization of the ability of PcG proteins to inhibit remodeling than was possible with PRC1 (Shao et al., 1999). The experiments described below characterize the ability of PCC and PSC to bind DNA and to interact with nucleosomal arrays to prevent remodeling.
