## PCC and PSC Bind DNA
The PcG proteins present in PCC have not been demonstrated to bind DNA, although one mammalian PSC homolog, MEL-18, has (Kanno et al., 1995). The ability of PCC and PSC to inhibit remodeling on arrays but not on mononucleosomes suggested that they might recognize free DNA. We tested PCC and PSC for DNA binding activity using filter binding with a 155 bp probe and found that both PCC and PSC bind DNA with high affinity (Figure 4). The measured ${\sf K}_{\sf D}{\sf s}$ of PCC and PSC for DNA were similar $(\mathsf{P C C}=0.13\pm0.08\mathsf{n M}$ , $\mathsf{P S C}=0.15\pm0.08$ nM). We were able to use the filter binding assay to determine what fraction of molecules in each of our protein preparations were active for DNA binding. A high fraction of the molecules in PCC and PSC preparations were active for DNA binding (PCC $30\%-60\%$ , PSC $20\%$ – $50\%$ ), assuming binding as monomers. The concentrations of PSC and PCC stated in the results are based on these calculations.
We also detected DNA binding activity in preparations of isolated $\mathsf{P c}$ and PH, but the active fraction of different preparations was highly variable and, for most preparations, less than $10\%$ (data not shown). These data allowed us to reevaluate the ability of $\mathsf{P c}$ and PH to inhibit SWI/SNF-based remodeling in the REA assay. When corrected for the fraction of protein active for DNA binding in each preparation, Pc had similar ability to inhibit remodeling as PSC, and PH was 2- to 4-fold less active.
