To further characterize the effects of PcG proteins on access to the chromatin template, studies were performed with additional restriction enzymes. Four enzymes were tested (see Figure 2A), each of which has a different level of background cutting, presumably reflecting the frequency of templates in which it is occluded by a nucleosome. PSC and PCC were found to have minimal effects on the extent of digestion in the absence of hSWI/SNF (Figures 5B and 5C, left panels). For SacI, which is largely nonoccluded, little change in digestion was observed when restriction enzyme and hSWI/SNF were added simultaneously. Note that these results contrast with those obtained in Figure 5A in which remodeling was allowed to occur prior to restriction enzyme digestion. For the other three sites (HhaI, HaeIII, and XbaI), hSWI/SNF increased the extent of digestion; in each case, this increase was largely blocked by either PSC or PCC (Figures 5B and 5C). PCC and PSC seem to specifically block SWI/SNF-induced changes in digestion since digestion levels returned to those observed in the absence of hSWI/SNF. This is also consistent with nucleosomal positions remaining unaltered in the presence of PCC and PSC.
## PCC and PSC Block Chromatin Remodeling without Grossly Altering Nucleosome Position
To examine whether inhibition of remodeling by hSWI/ SNF is accompanied by changes in nucleosome organization, we examined the effect of PRC1, PCC, and PSC on MNase digestion of nucleosomal arrays. MNase preferentially digests the linker DNA between nucleosomes. On arrays of nucleosomes that are positioned by 5S sequences, digestion with MNase produces a distinct ladder, which can be visualized by probing Southern blots of the digested array with the 5S sequence. Incubation of the array with hSWI/SNF and ATP causes a smearing of the banding pattern (Jaskelioff et al., 2000; Kal et al., 2000) reflecting randomization of nucleosome positioning (Figures 6A and 6B). PRC1 (Figure 6A), PCC, and PSC (Figure 6B) block disruption of the MNase ladder by hSWI/SNF. Significantly, none of these PcG proteins or complexes altered the MNase digestion pattern of the array, implying that MNase was still able to access the array and that nucleosome organization was not grossly altered by the presence of these PcG proteins (Figures 6A and 6B). Together with the results in Figure 5A, these results suggest that complexing these PcG proteins with the template prevents hSWI/SNF-mediated nucleosome movement but not nuclease access to the template.
In the MNase experiments described above, it was possible that PCC or PSC altered nucleosome positions but nonetheless maintained a regular organization of nucleosomes on the template. To determine whether nucleosome positions were altered, we carried out indirect end labeling after digestion with MNase or DNaseI and compared nucleosome position in the presence or absence of PCC or PSC. As shown in Figures 6C and 6D, neither PSC not PCC altered nucleosome position as assayed by either MNase or DNaseI digestion, both of which yield nucleosomal ladders due to the tight positioning of the nucleosomes on the 5S array. Similar results were obtained in MNase experiments with PRC1 (data not shown). Interestingly, however, arrays incubated with PCC or PSC were approximately $5-$ to 10- fold less sensitive to digestion by MNase, but about 10- fold more sensitive to digestion by DNase I. Although we do not yet have an explanation for this observation, it is consistent with PCC or PSC altering the structure
