## PSC and PCC Prevent hSWI/SNF from Binding to the Template
PSC and PCC could prevent remodeling by preventing hSWI/SNF from binding to the template, by interfering with remodeling of chromatin by bound hSWI/SNF, or by a combination of both mechanisms. To determine whether binding of PSC or PCC to the template interferes with the binding of hSWI/SNF, we carried out in vitro chromatin immunoprecipitations (ChIPs) using antisera to BRG1, the major ATPase subunit and remodeling protein of hSWI/SNF. We compared the amount of DNA precipitated by anti-BRG1 on templates incubated with or without PSC or PCC and correlated these results with inhibition of remodeling in the same reactions (Figure 7). When arrays were preincubated with concentrations of PSC or PCC that inhibit chromatin remodeling, the amount of BRG1 bound to the template was decreased by at least 10-fold (Figure 7A). Titrations of PSC and PCC indicate that exclusion of BRG1 from the template correlates with inhibition of remodeling (Figures 7A and 7B). Thus, these experiments suggest that PSC or PCC can exclude hSWI/SNF from a nucleosomal array, and are consistent with exclusion accounting, at least in part, for inhibition of remodeling.
## Discussion
We found that PH, PSC, Pc, and dRING1, the four PcG proteins present in PRC1, form a stable complex (PCC) that shares the ability of PRC1 to inhibit chromatin remodeling. Our results, together with previous studies of PcG protein interactions in Drosophila and mammals, suggest that these four proteins form an evolutionarily conserved module that constitutes the core of the PRC1 family of PcG complexes. We also show that a single subunit, PSC, is nearly as active as the core complex. The core complex and the single subunit PSC inhibit chromatin remodeling via a mechanism that does not completely occlude template access, but can exclude remodeling complexes. The ability to form a template structure that is refractory to remodeling complexes might be a key aspect of the mechanisms used by this family of PcG complexes to maintain a repressed state.
Genetic analysis of PcG function has provided a wealth of information regarding the characteristics of PcG-mediated silencing, but biochemical understanding of silencing mechanisms is still at the earliest stages. Purification of PcG complexes has proven to be extremely difficult, with conventional chromatography not producing sufficient yields for biochemical assays. The generation of fly strains carrying epitope-tagged PcG proteins that can be used in subsequent purification has allowed the isolation and characterization of PcG complexes (Ng et al., 2000; Shao et al., 1999; Tie et al., 2001). The development of a minimal, fully recombinant system that shares many of the activities of the endogenous complex and that can be readily produced in large quantities should facilitate detailed biochemical and structural analyses of interactions between chromatin and the PcG proteins found in PRC1.
The stable binding of high levels of PcG proteins to their target genes has led to the suggestion that PcG complexes are important components of a repressed chromatin structure (Paro, 1990). How PcG proteins interact with the components of chromatin and the nature of the chromatin structure formed when these proteins are bound are therefore likely to be important to understanding the mechanisms of repression. The finding that PSC and PCC inhibit remodeling of mononucleosomes inefficiently (Figure 2D) combined with their high affinity for bare DNA (Figure 4) raises the possibility that these proteins interact with chromatin, at least in part, by recognizing DNA. Analysis of the chromatin structure of PSC and PCC bound templates indicates that these proteins do not prevent digestion of DNA by nucleases, which is consistent with previous analyses of restriction enzyme and Dam accessibility at PcG-repressed genes (Boivin and Dura, 1998; Schlossherr et al., 1994). Although we expect that the chromatin structure at PcGrepressed genes in vivo is more complicated than the defined nucleosomal arrays used here, these results are consistent with the DNA remaining accessible in PcG bound chromatin (Figures 5, 6).
The observation that preincubation with PSC or PCC prevents BRG1 from being crosslinked to the template (Figure 7) suggests that the inhibition of remodeling occurs, at least in part, at the step of template binding. These data suggest a model in which PcG proteins and hSWI/SNF may not be able to cooccupy nucleosomal arrays, even when PcG proteins are present at low ratios to nucleosomes. This could be due simply to steric interference between large complexes; however, PCC behaves as a 440 kDa complex in glycerol gradient sedimentation (unpublished data), which is more than four times smaller than hSWI/SNF. Alternatively, PSC or PCC may occlude specific features of the array that are recognized by hSWI/SNF. For example, hSWI/SNF, PSC, and PCC all bind bare DNA (Figure 4; Quinn et al., 1996); one can therefore speculate that PSC and PCC may compete with hSWI/SNF for binding to the DNA component of nucleosomal arrays. It is also possible that PSC and PCC can exclude hSWI/SNF by altering the higherorder structure of the arrays. Specifically, the finding that PSC and PCC can act at ratios of less than one per nucleosome raises the possibility that they can simultaneously contact more than one nucleosome (or more than one linker), which might be expected to alter the structure of the array, perhaps making it difficult for SWI/SNF to recognize its substrate.
We find that PSC or PCC can exclude BRG1 in hSWI/ SNF from nucleosomal templates, yet in vivo experiments demonstrate colocalization of Trx and Pc at some loci (Chinwalla et al., 1995; Orlando et al., 1998). It is not known whether BRM (the Drosophila BRG1 homolog) colocalizes with PcG proteins; similarly, we do not know whether PcG proteins would exclude Trx from a nucleosomal template. Furthermore, the in vivo experiments look at populations of chromosomes, so that it is difficult to verify that colocalization actually occurs on the same DNA molecule. These data suggest, however, that although PcG proteins may be able to exclude certain trxG complexes from chromatin, there are other mechanisms involved in the antagonistic regulation of gene expression by the PcG and trxG.
PSC is active as an isolated polypeptide, raising the possibility that this protein is an important component of PCC activity and could function in the absence of the other PCC subunits. Indeed, recent evidence suggests PSC is an essential component of the silencing mechanism and may function independently of the other PCC subunits in certain circumstances. Beuchle et al. (2001) removed PcG genes by recombination in imaginal disc clones and monitored the effect on homeotic gene expression. Loss of PSC and its homolog suppressor of zeste 2 $[\mathsf{S u}(Z)2]$ or PH, but not of other PcG proteins, resulted in rapid upregulation of homeotic genes. Repression could be restored by resupply of PSC [and Su(Z)2] provided only a few cell generations had passed (Beuchle et al., 2001). These results are consistent with an essential role for PSC in the actual silencing mechanism. Second, immunocytochemistry in Drosophila embryos demonstrates that the majority of PH, Pc, and PSC dissociate from chromosomes during mitosis; PSC reassociates with the chromosomes before Pc and PH, suggesting it may function independently of these subunits in vivo during reestablishment of repression (Buchenau et al., 1998). Because we also detect some activity in preparations of Pc and PH, it seems likely that these proteins can contribute to inhibition of remodeling by PCC. Indeed, preliminary data suggest that PSC does not account for all of the activity of PCC (unpublished data). The possibility that the function of at least some subunits of PcG complexes is redundant is consistent with the ability of duplications of certain PcG proteins to compensate for mutations in others (Cheng et al., 1994).
In vivo, PcG proteins are targeted to appropriate genes by PREs, but PCC does not require PRE sequences to prevent chromatin remodeling. Preliminary results with templates that include PRE sequences from the Ubx gene suggest that PREs may not target PCC and PSC in vitro (unpublished data). This raises the possibility that the basic mechanisms by which PcG proteins influence chromatin can be mechanistically separated from those which target repression to appropriate genes. It seems likely that PcG proteins interact, perhaps directly, with DNA binding factors that target them to PREs. The characterization of PCC activity provides a system in which targeting of PcG proteins can be analyzed in vitro.
## Experimental Procedures
## Protein Expression and Purification
Flag epitope-tagged PH-p, PSC, Pc, and dRING1 were expressed in Sf9 cells using the bac-to-bac system (Gibco) as described (Phelan et al., 1999). M2 anti-Flag beads (Sigma) were used for all purifications. Proteins were bound to M2 beads in $300~\mathsf{m M}$ KCl for 4 hr at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with $0.05\%$ NP40 ( $10\upmu\mathsf{M}Z\mathsf{n C l}_{2}$ was added to some PH, PSC, dRING1, and PCC purifications and increased column yields). Beads were washed with BC buffer $(20~\mathsf{m M}$ Hepes $[\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}8.0]$ , $0.2\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}$ EDTA, $20\%$ Glycerol, $0.2~\mathsf{m M}$ PMSF, and $0.5~\mathsf{m M}$ DTT) containing $0.1\%$ NP40 and up to 2 M KCl, and proteins were eluted for at least 1 hr with $0.4\mathrm{\mg/ml}$ Flag peptide (DYKDDDDK) in BC300, $0.1\%$ NP40. PSC and dRING1 were purified directly from nuclear extracts by anti-Flag affinity. PCC was purified from nuclear extracts from Sf9 cells infected with Flag-PH, PSC, dRING1, and Pc viruses by antiFlag affinity. PH was purified from whole cell extracts by $\mathsf{Q}$ -sepharose (Pharmacia) $(500~\mathsf{m M}$ KCl elution) followed by anti-Flag chromatography. Pc was purified from whole cell or nuclear extracts by Biorex-70 (Bio-Rad) $(850\mathsf{m M}$ KCl elution) followed by anti-Flag chromatography. Protease inhibitors were used in all purifications at the following concentrations: Aprotinin $10\ \upmu{\sf g}/\sf m l$ , Leupeptin $10\ \upmu{\sf g}/\sf m l$ , Pepstatin $2\ \upmu{\sf g}/\sf m l$ , TLCK $50\ \upmu{\sf g}/\sf m\upmu$ , and $1.6\ \upmu\mathfrak{g}/\mathfrak{m}\mathfrak{l}$ Benzamidine. Protein concentrations were determined by Bradford assay and purity was estimated from colloidal coomassie (Novagen) and silver stained protein gels.
## Glycerol Gradient Purification of PSC
PSC ( $10{-}20~|\Im|$ was dialyzed into 2 M Urea/300 mM KCl glycerol gradient buffer $(50~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris $\left[\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 8.0\right]$ and 1 mM EDTA) for $4h r$ at room temperature in a Slide-A-Lyzer cassette (Pierce). Dialyzed PSC was loaded onto a $5\mathrm{\ml}5\%-30\%$ glycerol gradient in the above conditions and centrifuged at 35,000 RPM in a Sw55Ti rotor for $12-16\ h r$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Gradients were resolved into $200\mathrm{-}250~\upmu\upmu$ fractions and adjacent fractions were pooled, dialyzed into BC100 with $0.1\%$ NP40, and stored at $\mathtt{-80^{\circ}C}$ .
## Plasmid Supercoiling Assay
Plasmid supercoiling assays were carried out essentially as described in Shao et al. (1999) with chromatin assembled with Xenopus heat-treated oocyte extracts, except that reaction volumes were reduced to 10 or $20\ \upmu\up l.$ Reactions contained 3–5 ng of internally labeled, assembled plasmid and up to 600 ng of hSWI/SNF. Preincubation was for 15–30 min at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and remodeling reactions were for $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## 5S Array Assembly
The 5S array construction has been described (Utley et al., 1998). Nucleosomal arrays were assembled with purified HeLa core histones by gradient salt dialysis as described (Sif et al., 2001). Assembly was confirmed by MNase analysis and digestion with EcoRI which cuts between the 5S repeats, allowing unassembled and assembled fragments to be resolved by agarose gel electrophoresis.
## Restriction Enzyme Accessibility Assays
The following conditions were used for restriction enzyme accessibility assays (REAs): $12\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}$ Hepes, $\boldsymbol{0.3\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}}$ EDTA, 10 mM Tris, 120 ${\mathsf{n g}}/{\upmu\mathsf{I}}$ BSA, $13\%$ Glycerol, $0.2~\mathsf{m M}$ DTT, 4 mM $M g C l_{2},$ and $2~\mathsf{m M}$ ATP. 1 ng $(7.9\:\mathrm{fm})$ of $^{32}{\mathsf{P}}$ -labeled 5S array and 9 ng (71 fm) of HeLa polynucleosomes were added to $10~\upmu\upmu\upmu$ reactions (polynculeosomes were added to increase overall nucleosome concentration; similar results were obtained with the 5S array alone). $\mathsf{P c G}$ proteins were diluted in BC300 with $1\mathrm{mg/ml}$ BSA and $0.1\%$ NP40. Following a 15 min preincubation with PcG proteins at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , hSWI/SNF was added at concentrations determined to be saturating for remodeling activity (70–250 ng per reaction). Four units HhaI (New England Biolabs) were added with hSWI/SNF, reactions were allowed to proceed for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and then stopped with $2\upmu\upmu$ DSB $50~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris, $100~\mathrm{{m}}\mathrm{{M}}$ EDTA, $1\%$ SDS, $25\%$ glycerol, bromophenol blue, and xylene cyanol). In Figure 3A, reactions containing 8, 16, 24, or 32 nM nucleosomes (1 ng 5S array $^+$ Hela polynucleosomes to the indicated concentration) contained 4, 8, 12, or 16 U of HhaI, and 250 or 500 ng of hSWI/SNF. Five units of SacI and HaeIII (NEB) and $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10\ U}}$ of XbaI (Amersham) were used in the experiments shown in Figures 5A and 5B. Reactions were digested with $20\ {\upmu\up g}$ proteinase K for $30\mathrm{-}60\mathrm{\min}$ at $55^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , resolved on $1\%$ agarose 1X TAE gels, dried, and exposed to a PhosphorImager (Molecular Dynamics) screen for quantitation. hSWI/ SNF was prepared as described (Sif et al., 1998); hSWI/SNF preparations were $50\%-70\%$ pure by silver stain.
Mononucleosome restriction enzyme accessibility assays were carried out essentially as described in G. Narlikar et al. (submitted). In brief, mononucleosomes were assembled by gradient dialysis onto the 155 bp TPT fragment that was body-labeled by PCR. PSC or PCC were preincubated with mononucleosomes at 1 nM for 15 min at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ followed by addition of saturating amounts of hSWI/ SNF and PstI for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Reactions were stopped and digested with Proteinase K as described for experiments with the 5S array, separated on $5\%$ acrylamide gels in 0.5X TBE, and quantitated by PhosphorImager.
## Filter Binding
Double filter binding analysis of DNA binding (Wong and Lohman, 1993) was carried with a gel-purified, body-labeled TPT fragment essentially as described (G. Narlikar et al., submitted). PcG proteins were titrated into $20~\upmu\upmu\upmu$ binding reactions (conditions as described above for REA analysis) in excess to DNA and were incubated at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $45\mathrm{\min}$ (similar values were obtained using a 15 min incubation). Filters were quantitated by PhosphorImager, and binding constants were obtained from first-order fits to the data using KaleidaGraph (R values for fits were typically ${\tt>}0.95)$ ). The active concentration of PcG protein preparations was determined by quantifying the amount of binding at concentrations of TPT at least 50- fold higher than the measured $\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{D}}$ .
## MNase/DNaseI Assays
MNase experiments were carried out under conditions similar to those used in REAs with the following modifications: reaction volumes were $60~\upmu\upmu\up$ , and 9 ng of unlabeled 5S array template $(3{\mathsf{n g}}/$ lane) were used in each reaction. ATP concentration was $4~\mathsf{m M}$ , $\mathsf{M g C l}_{2}$ was $7~\mathsf{m M}$ , and remodeling was allowed to proceed for 30 min. MNase analysis was carried out as described (Sif et al., 2001). Digestion of arrays incubated with PcG proteins required at least 5-fold higher MNase concentrations than control reactions (Figure 6). For indirect end labeling analysis, reactions were scaled up to $100~\upmu\upmu\upmu$ containing 25 ng of template. The probe corresponds to the ClaI end of the 5S array (Utley at al., 1998), which is outside of the 5S repeats and is therefore a unique sequence in the array; similar results were also obtained by direct visualization of end-labeled template (data not shown). DNaseI assays were carried out similarly to MNase assays with Promega RQ1 DNaseI and DNaseI dilution buffer $(5\%$ glycerol, 10 mM NaCl, and $40~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris $\left[\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 8.0\right]$ ; $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ added to $40~\mathsf{m M}$ before digestion) in place of MNase buffer.
## Chromatin Immunoprecipitations
For chromatin immunoprecipitations (ChIPs), REA reactions were scaled up 4-fold. Template was incubated for $15\mathrm{\min}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with PSC, PCC, or their dilution buffer, followed by addition of hSWI/ SNF for an additional 15 min. Four microliters were removed for REA analysis of remodeling and formaldehyde was added to $1\%$ to the rest of the reaction for 15 min at room temperature. After addition of Glycine to $0.125~\mathrm{mM}$ for 5 min, reactions were diluted to $150~\upmu\upiota$ with BC100 ( $10\%$ glycerol) and digested for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ at $\mathsf{37^{\circ}C}$ with EcoRI. 1 $5~{\upmu\upuparrow}10\times$ cell lysis buffer $(1\times=0.1\%$ deoxycholate, 1 mM EDTA, $50~\mathsf{m M}$ Hepes [pH 7.6], 140 mM NaCl, $1\%$ Triton $x-100$ ) and 2 ${\upmu\mathsf{g}}$ HeLa polynucleosomes were added. Reactions were precleared protein A beads (Sigma) and incubated $4-12\mathsf{h r}$ with ${\mathfrak{a}}_{\upmu\upiota}$ anti-BRG1 antisera. Antibody complexes were captured with $25\ \upmu\up$ protein A beads for 1 hr at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , followed by extensive washing $(2\times$ lysis buffer, $2\times$ lysis buffer with $500~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}$ NaCl, $2\times$ BufferIII [0.25 M LiCl, $0.5\%$ NP40, $0.5\%$ deoxycholate, 1 mM EDTA, $10~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris (pH 8)], 2X TE). DNA was eluted from beads in $50~\upmu\updownarrow$ EB ( $10~\mathsf{m M}$ EDTA, $1\%$ SDS, $50~\mathsf{m M}$ Tris [pH 8]) for 10 min at $65^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , followed by a second elution in $150~\upmu|~0.67\%$ SDS in TE for 5 min at ${\mathfrak{65^{\circ}C}}$ . NaCl was added to pooled elutions to $200~\mathsf{m M}$ and crosslinks reversed by incubation overnight at $\mathtt{65^{\circ}C}$ . Reactions were digested with Proteinase K, extracted and precipitated with tRNA. Denatured samples were applied to GeneScreen membrane using a slot blotter (Hoefer Scientific) and hybridized with the 5S probe.
